  "methods_multiplex_paragraphs": [
    {
      "method_paragraph_index": 17,
      "text": "To screen individual C1498-FLARE founder populations for copies of FLARE-miniV10-10X-UMI integrations gDNA was collected and founder tags were amplified and prepared into sequencing libraries. Founder tag libraries for individual populations were multiplexed and sequenced on an Illumina MiniSeq with a Mid-Ouput 300-cycle kit. Founder tags for each clone were identified as having more than 4300 reads. Cells from each clone were expanded, aliquoted, and cryopreserved."
    },
    {
      "method_paragraph_index": 20,
      "text": "High molecular weight (HMW) gDNA was isolated from parental C1498 cells and individually engineered clones using the Monarch HMW gDNA Kit for Cells (Cat #T3050S). HMW gDNA samples were multiplexed and prepared into a sequencing library following the Oxford Nanopore SQK-NBD114.24 kit. The library was then sequenced on a PromethION flow cell with R10.4.1 chemistry. Sequencing data were basecalled and demultiplexed using Guppy. Variants were called using PEPPER-Margin-DeepVariant, SNPs were isolated and filtered for a minimum quality of 20 and mean depth between 10 and 80. To remove population variants, the GRCm39 dbSNP was annotated using Ensembl VEP and variants with moderate or high impact were removed. The resulting dbSNP was subtracted from filtered SNPs using Bedtools (version 2.31.0)114. Remaining variants were annotated with Ensembl VEP and SIFT and intersected with datasets of known AML mutations."
    },
    {
      "method_paragraph_index": 22,
      "text": "Two flasks were maintained in normal culture media; the remaining two flasks were maintained in media containing 0.05ug/ml AraC. Flasks were maintained for 43 days to allow for the development of AraC resistance. Media was replaced 2–3 times/week and split when needed. Dose-response curves were calculated for each flask on day 15 of treatment, and a final dose-response curve was calculated for flask displaying resistance on day 36 of treatment. On day 43 of treatment, cells were prepared for MULTIseq tagging and single cell capture following MULTIseq and 10x protocols."
    },
    {
      "method_paragraph_index": 24,
      "text": "Engineered lineage tracing HL60-FLARE cells were sorted into 96-well plates at 100 cells/well following electroporation with FLARE-pbCas9-tag-mCherry to establish GESTALT lineage tracing populations, as described above. Sorted well populations were expanded for 23 days. On day 23 post FACS, five expanded wells were combined into an initial cell pool. The pool was then split for 10x scRNA-seq preparation, methanol fixation, cryopreservation, and in vitro AraC treatment. For in vitro AraC treatment cell pools were split into control (no AraC) and AraC conditions (0.01 μg/mL AraC). All treatment wells were cultured in duplicates. AraC solutions and media were made fresh for media changes twice per week, and wells were split as needed. Cell concentration for each well was measured once per week in duplicates using the Luna II Cell Counter. Cells were regularly cryopreserved through the experiment for later analysis. In vitro treatment continued for 89 days, after which cells were prepared for 10x scRNA-seq preparation with MULTIseq multiplexing."
    },
    {
      "method_paragraph_index": 27,
      "text": "Cells were captured for scRNA-seq following 10x guidelines for 3’ scRNA-seq with feature barcoding. For multiplexed single cell experiments, cells were first tagged following MULTIseq protocols (Millipore Sigma, LMO001)115. Gene expression and MULTIseq libraries were constructed and quantified according to 10x and MULTIseq guidelines, with one alteration: a portion of cDNA was set aside following initial cDNA amplification but prior to SPRIselect cleanup for use in FLARE lineage library construction. This was done to avoid splitting FLARE transcripts between GEX and feature barcoding fractions."
    },
    {
      "method_paragraph_index": 29,
      "text": "For bulk DNA sequencing raw data were converted to fastq format using bcl2fastq2 (v2.20.0.422) or BCL Convert (Illumina, v4.3.13). For scRNA-seq raw data were converted to fastq format using CellRanger mkfastq. Cells were then called using CellRanger count for non-multiplexed 10x runs or CellRanger multi for MULTIseq multiplexed 10x runs116."
    },
    {
      "method_paragraph_index": 30,
      "text": "Cell Ranger output filtered matrices were analyzed with Seurat (v5.1.0)117. Low quality cells were filtered out based on the number of RNA features and percentage of mitochondrial reads. To isolate singlets in non-multiplexed samples, doublets were identified and removed using the scDblFinder R package (v1.20.2)118. For multiplexed samples, MULTIseq barcode filtered matrices were added as Assay Objects to Seurat objects. Cells were demultiplexed and singlets were identified and isolated with the MULTIseqDemux() function. We additionally used singleCode119 to assess our ability to detect doublets with lineage barcode data. Although sensitivity was low, 83% of detected doublets were previously established doublets using MULTIseq tags. For PBMC and bone marrow samples cell type annotations were added using the SingleR R package (v2.8.0) with the MouseRNAseqData reference set from the celldex R package (v1.16.0)120. All single cell Seurat objects were normalized with the SCTransform R package (v0.4.1) for data visualization and integration with other objects121,122. Differential gene expression analysis was conducted using the FindMarkers() and FindAllMarkers() Seurat functions."
    }
  ],
